Wlabel = "TelnetU Help"
Contents = TelnetU,TelnetU Keys,TelnetU Commands
Name = TelnetU
Llabel = " TELNETU"
Slabel = "TELNETU"
Branch = TelnetU Keys,TelnetU Commands
Title = "TelnetU - VT100/UTF-8 Terminal"
Text =
TelnetU logs you in to another computer on the network, as \
telnet does, but it has a terminal of its own: a VT100 and \
xterm, with UTF-8. Box lines, blocks, accents, Greek and \
Cyrillic letters, and the art of BBSes (CP437) come out right. \
So today's telnet hosts look as they should: BBSes, MUDs, \
nethack.alt.org, telehack.com, mapscii.me.

Type the name or the address of the host, then press Enter. \
Press Cmd for the list of known hosts. Names that are not in \
/etc/hosts are looked up with DNS. With no name, you get the \
telnet> prompt.

TelnetU opens a window of its own: 80 columns by 24 lines. \
When you leave it, you come back here.

In a UNIX System window, type: telnetu host [port]. \
The old telnet is still there: telnet host. \EOT\

Name = TelnetU Keys
Llabel = " TELNETU  KEYS"
Slabel = "  KEYS"
Branch = TelnetU,TelnetU Commands
Title = "TelnetU Keys"
Text =
Exit gives the telnet> prompt at the bottom of the window. \
There, Return takes you back to the host, and Exit again \
closes the connection and the window.

^] does the same as Exit. So does ~ at the start of a line: \
~quit closes the connection, and ~~ sends one ~.

Help lists the telnet> commands. Rfrsh draws the screen again. \
Break sends a telnet BREAK to the host.

The arrow keys, Page, Shift-Page, Home, End and F1 to F8 go to \
the host. Shift-F1 to Shift-F8 are F9 to F16. \
A mouse click goes to hosts that ask for the mouse. \EOT\

Name = TelnetU Commands
Llabel = " TELNETUCOMMANDS"
Slabel = "COMMANDS"
Branch = TelnetU,TelnetU Keys
Title = "TelnetU Commands"
Text =
At the telnet> prompt, type a command and press Return:

open host [port]   connect to a host
close              close the connection
quit               close it and leave TelnetU
z                  a UNIX shell (type exit to come back)
send x             ayt brk ip ao ec el eof nop escape
charset c          auto utf8 latin1 cp437
term types         the terminal types to give the host
status             what is going on
?                  all the commands \EOT\
